; nyrqis.def -- the export table of the boot's SECOND provider, stated as
; ordinals rather than left to the linker's default.
;
; GNU ld's PE backend numbers exports from 1 by default, which would make
; "ordinal 1" and "EAT index 0" the same number -- and a `GetProcAddress`
; that treated the Windows ordinal form as a bare index would then answer
; every question correctly. The ordinal form exists because the two are NOT
; the same: Windows computes `ordinal - OrdinalBase`, and the provider
; declares the base. So this provider declares 10, and `NyrqisBuild` and
; `NyrqisMode` are ordinals 10 and 11 (EAT slots 0 and 1). The boot asks for
; both, requires exactly the addresses their NAMES answer, and requires
; 9 (below the base) and 12 (past the last slot) to resolve to nothing --
; which is a question a base-1 fixture could not put to the implementation.
;
; ld reads this file as an input beside the object; the ordinals are also the
; pin `build.sh` checks out of the linked DLL (`Export Address Table --
; Ordinal Base 10`), so a linker that ignored the base would fail the build.
;
; The last two entries are FORWARDED exports, not code: `= module.export` makes
; ld write the string into the export directory instead of an RVA into .text.
; `NyrqisTick` names kernel32.dll's own GetTickCount, so `GetProcAddress` on it
; must answer THAT module's real address (and the client can call through it);
; `NyrqisGhost` names an export kernel32.dll does not have, so the same chase
; must end in ERROR_PROC_NOT_FOUND rather than in this image. They are ordinals
; 12 and 13, which keeps the EAT contiguous and moves the "past the last slot"
; probe from 12 to 14. `build.sh` pins both forwarder strings out of
; `objdump -p`, so a linker that stopped writing forwarders fails there first.
;
; The third forwarder is what a RUNTIME row makes observable: `NyrqisDyn`
; names `nyrqis_dyn.dll.NyrqisDynValue`, and that module is a file on the
; volume that nothing loads until the ring-3 client calls LoadLibraryA. So the
; SAME GetProcAddress question must answer ERROR_PROC_NOT_FOUND before the
; load, the target's real address after it, and ERROR_PROC_NOT_FOUND again
; after FreeLibrary -- three different answers from one EAT slot, decided by
; rows that only exist while the module is loaded. It is ordinal 14, and the
; EAT is contiguous again, so the "past the last slot" probe is 15.
LIBRARY nyrqis.dll
EXPORTS
NyrqisBuild @10
NyrqisMode @11
NyrqisTick = kernel32.dll.GetTickCount @12
NyrqisGhost = kernel32.dll.NoSuchExport @13
NyrqisDyn = nyrqis_dyn.dll.NyrqisDynValue @14
